-- CONTROL for i4-xmod-requires-foreign-iface-rejected.  Two edits from the sibling,
-- not one, and the second is forced rather than chosen: `zmod` gains its own
-- `impl Sizer Int`, and `amod`'s impl moves from head `Int` to head `Bool` because
-- otherwise COHERENCE rejects the file on both arms (`T-CONFLICTING-IMPL`) before the
-- obligation channel is reached at all — see `amod.mdk`, where that is derived.
-- Stated here too because "one-token control" is a claim this fixture CANNOT make.
--
-- SPEC ANSWER, HAND-DERIVED, and the VALUE is what makes this a control rather than a
-- second copy: `match(IE, zmod.Sizer Int)` is now the singleton {`zmod`'s impl},
-- trivially its own ⊑-minimum, so §3 `inst` selects it and `bulk 3` = 3 + 7 = 10.
-- `debug (Wrap 3)` therefore prints `W10`.
--
-- 🚨 `W10` IS A VALUE ONLY `zmod`'s IMPL CAN PRODUCE, which is the half a
-- verdict-only control cannot express.  If a future change makes the `requires`
-- channel spelling-keyed again, the sibling flips to ACCEPT; this row would keep
-- printing `W10` (its goal is unambiguous), so the PAIR separates "the reject was
-- removed" from "the wrong impl was selected".
--
-- ⚠️ THIS ROW BEHAVES IDENTICALLY ON BOTH ARMS, deliberately, and stated so it is not
-- mistaken for coverage of #1446: a program whose goal has exactly one candidate is
-- not a witness to an identity re-key.  Its job is to rule out the duller readings of
-- its sibling's reject — "a conditional instance cannot discharge `requires` across
-- modules", "any same-spelled interface anywhere in the graph poisons the goal" —
-- each of which predicts a reject HERE too, and neither survives `W10`.
import amod
import zmod.{Wrap(..)}

main = println (debug (Wrap 3))
